# parallel_alias_nested_rowmajor — the shape the matmul benchmark depends on:
# a nested loop inside `parallel for`, writing one row-major array read back at the
# same index.
#
# `m[i * 8 + j]` is the write index and the read index, spelled identically in both
# places, so the read is of the element this iteration owns.  What makes this worth
# its own case is that the index is not the loop variable itself but an expression
# of two of them: a rule that compared "is the index a bare loop variable" would
# accept this by accident, and one that compared text would reject `i * 8 + j`
# written two different ways.  Both directions are wrong; the rule compares the
# expression.
#
# If this ever stops compiling, `bench/matmul.vel` stops compiling with it.

def main() -> None {
    mut m: Array[int, 64]
    parallel for i in range(0, 8) {
        for j in range(0, 8) {
            m[i * 8 + j] = m[i * 8 + j] + 1
        }
    }
    print(m[0], m[63])
}
